      "reviewStatus": "pass",
      "question": "In the stomach, H+ ions are secreted in exchange for which of the following ions?",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": "Na+"
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": "K+"
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "text": "Ca2+"
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "Cl-"
        }
      ],
      "correctOption": 2,
      "questionOptionFlags": [],
      "explanationFlags": [
        {
          "field": "explanation",
          "severity": 3,
          "reasons": [
            "edge_noise"
          ],
          "suspectLines": [
            {
              "line": 1,
              "text": "H+ ions are secreted into the lumen of the stomach in exchange for K+ by the H+-K+ pump (H+, K+-ATPase).",
              "reasons": [
                "edge_noise"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "H+ ions are secreted into the lumen of the stomach in exchange for K+ by the H+-K+ pump (H+, K+-ATPase).\n\nMechanism of HCl formation:\n• In the parietal cells, CO2 and H2O are converted to H+ and HCO3-, catalyzed by carbonic anhydrase.\n• H+ is secreted into the lumen of the stomach by the H+-K+ pump in exchange for K+.\n• HCO3- moves out of the cell into the bloodstream in exchange for Cl- (Cl-/HCO3- exchange). This Cl- moves into the lumen of the stomach.\n• H+ and Cl- combine to form HCl.\n\nAs HCO3- is added to the venous blood, the pH of the blood increases. This is called alkaline tide. Eventually, this HCO3- will be secreted in the pancreatic secretions to neutralize H+ in the small intestine.\n\nProton pump inhibitors inhibit the H+-K+ pump."
    },
